---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { loadContent } from "../../lib/content";
import { calBookingUrl } from "../../lib/site";

const { provider } = loadContent();
---

<Layout
  title={`Federalism | CBSE Class 10 Civics | ${provider.name}`}
  description="CBSE Class 10 Civics Chapter 2 revision notes on Federalism. Key features, coming together vs holding together, three legislative lists (Union, State, Concurrent), linguistic states, language policy, decentralisation (1992 amendment), and an interactive 20-question board quiz."
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <nav class="small" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/">Home</a> / <a href="/subjects/">Subjects</a> / <a href="/resources/">Resources</a> / Federalism
    </nav>

    <div class="resource-header">
      <span class="eyebrow">CBSE Class 10 · Social Science · Civics Chapter 2</span>
      <h1>Federalism</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Comprehensive revision notes on federal principles, two federation routes, India's three-fold legislative distribution (Union, State, Concurrent Lists), linguistic reorganization, 1992 local decentralisation, and an interactive 20-question board exam quiz.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Core Concepts -->
    <section class="section">
      <h2>Key Political Concepts &amp; Constitutional Framework</h2>

      <div class="grid two concept-grid">
        <article class="card">
          <h3>🏛️ What is Federalism &amp; Key Features</h3>
          <p class="formula">Two or more tiers of government with constitutional autonomy</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>Federalism:</strong> A system of government in which power is divided between a central authority and various constituent units of the country.</li>
            <li><strong>Key Features:</strong>
              <br/>1. Two or more levels (or tiers) of government.
              <br/>2. Different tiers govern the same citizens, but each tier has its own constitutional <strong>jurisdiction</strong> in legislation, taxation, and administration.
              <br/>3. Fundamental provisions of the Constitution cannot be unilaterally changed by one level of government; requires consent of both levels.
              <br/>4. Courts act as an umpire if disputes arise between different levels of government.
              <br/>5. Financial autonomy is guaranteed by clearly specified revenue sources for each tier.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <h3>🌐 Two Routes of Federation Formation</h3>
          <p class="formula">'Coming Together' vs. 'Holding Together'</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>1. 'Coming Together' Federations:</strong>
              <br/>• Independent states come together on their own to form a bigger unit to pool sovereignty and retain identity to increase security.
              <br/>• Constituent states usually have equal powers and are strong vis-a-vis the federal government.
              <br/>• Examples: <strong>USA, Switzerland, and Australia</strong>.
            </li>
            <li><strong>2. 'Holding Together' Federations:</strong>
              <br/>• A large country decides to divide its power between constituent states and the national government.
              <br/>• Central government tends to be more powerful vis-a-vis the states; some units have special powers.
              <br/>• Examples: <strong>India, Spain, and Belgium</strong>.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <h3>📜 What Makes India a Federal Country?</h3>
          <p class="formula">Three-Fold Distribution of Legislative Powers</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>1. Union List:</strong> Includes subjects of national importance (Defence, Foreign Affairs, Banking, Communications, Currency). Union Government alone can make laws.</li>
            <li><strong>2. State List:</strong> Contains subjects of State and local importance (Police, Trade, Commerce, Agriculture, Irrigation). State Governments alone can make laws.</li>
            <li><strong>3. Concurrent List:</strong> Includes subjects of common interest to both Union and States (Education, Forests, Trade Unions, Marriage, Adoption, Succession). Both can make laws; in case of conflict, the <strong>Union law prevails</strong>.</li>
            <li><strong>Residuary Subjects:</strong> Subjects that do not fall in any of the three lists (e.g., Computer software, cyber laws) fall under the exclusive jurisdiction of the <strong>Union Government</strong>.</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <h3>🏘️ Decentralisation in India (1992 Amendment)</h3>
          <p class="formula">73rd &amp; 74th Constitutional Amendments</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li>A major step toward decentralisation was taken in <strong>1992</strong>, making the third tier (Local Government) more powerful and effective:
              <br/>1. Mandatory to hold regular elections to local government bodies.
              <br/>2. Seats reserved in elected bodies for SCs, STs, and OBCs.
              <br/>3. At least <strong>one-third (33%) of all positions reserved for women</strong>.
              <br/>4. Independent <strong>State Election Commission</strong> created in each state to conduct panchayat and municipal elections.
              <br/>5. State governments required to share powers and revenue with local bodies.
            </li>
            <li><strong>Panchayati Raj:</strong> Gram Panchayat at village level, Panchayat Samiti at block level, and Zilla Parishad at district level. Urban areas have Municipalities and Municipal Corporations (headed by a Mayor).</li>
          </ul>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Visual Diagrams Section -->
    <section class="section">
      <h2>Visual Concepts: Three Legislative Lists &amp; Panchayati Raj Tiers</h2>

      <div class="grid two">
        <div class="card visual-card">
          <div class="visual-badge">Visual Diagram 1 · Three-Fold Legislative Distribution (7th Schedule)</div>
          <div class="svg-container">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 460 250" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" aria-label="Union State Concurrent lists">
              <rect width="460" height="250" fill="#f8fafc" rx="8"/>
              
              <!-- 3 Column Cards -->
              <!-- Union List -->
              <rect x="15" y="30" width="135" height="175" rx="6" fill="#eff6ff" stroke="#3b82f6" stroke-width="1.5"/>
              <text x="82" y="52" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" font-weight="700" fill="#1d4ed8">Union List</text>
              <line x1="25" y1="62" x2="140" y2="62" stroke="#bfdbfe" stroke-width="1"/>
              <text x="25" y="80" font-size="9" fill="#1e3a8a">• Defence</text>
              <text x="25" y="98" font-size="9" fill="#1e3a8a">• Foreign Affairs</text>
              <text x="25" y="116" font-size="9" fill="#1e3a8a">• Banking</text>
              <text x="25" y="134" font-size="9" fill="#1e3a8a">• Communications</text>
              <text x="25" y="152" font-size="9" fill="#1e3a8a">• Currency</text>
              <rect x="25" y="168" width="115" height="26" rx="4" fill="#3b82f6"/>
              <text x="82" y="184" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9" font-weight="700" fill="#fff">Union Govt Only</text>

              <!-- State List -->
              <rect x="162" y="30" width="135" height="175" rx="6" fill="#ecfdf5" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.5"/>
              <text x="230" y="52" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" font-weight="700" fill="#047857">State List</text>
              <line x1="172" y1="62" x2="287" y2="62" stroke="#a7f3d0" stroke-width="1"/>
              <text x="172" y="80" font-size="9" fill="#065f46">• Police</text>
              <text x="172" y="98" font-size="9" fill="#065f46">• Trade &amp; Commerce</text>
              <text x="172" y="116" font-size="9" fill="#065f46">• Agriculture</text>
              <text x="172" y="134" font-size="9" fill="#065f46">• Irrigation</text>
              <text x="172" y="152" font-size="9" fill="#065f46">• Prisons</text>
              <rect x="172" y="168" width="115" height="26" rx="4" fill="#10b981"/>
              <text x="230" y="184" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9" font-weight="700" fill="#fff">State Govt Only</text>

              <!-- Concurrent List -->
              <rect x="310" y="30" width="135" height="175" rx="6" fill="#fef3c7" stroke="#d97706" stroke-width="1.5"/>
              <text x="377" y="52" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" font-weight="700" fill="#b45309">Concurrent List</text>
              <line x1="320" y1="62" x2="435" y2="62" stroke="#fde68a" stroke-width="1"/>
              <text x="320" y="80" font-size="9" fill="#78350f">• Education</text>
              <text x="320" y="98" font-size="9" fill="#78350f">• Forests</text>
              <text x="320" y="116" font-size="9" fill="#78350f">• Trade Unions</text>
              <text x="320" y="134" font-size="9" fill="#78350f">• Marriage &amp; Adoption</text>
              <text x="320" y="152" font-size="9" fill="#78350f">• Succession</text>
              <rect x="320" y="168" width="115" height="26" rx="4" fill="#d97706"/>
              <text x="377" y="184" text-anchor="middle" font-size="8" font-weight="700" fill="#fff">Both (Union Prevails)</text>

              <text x="230" y="230" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10" fill="#64748b">Residuary Subjects (Cyber laws, IT) fall under exclusive Union jurisdiction.</text>
            </svg>
          </div>
          <p class="visual-caption">The Constitution clearly demarcates legislative domains into Union, State, and Concurrent Lists.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="card visual-card">
          <div class="visual-badge">Visual Diagram 2 · Rural Local Government (Panchayati Raj Architecture)</div>
          <div class="svg-container">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 460 250" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" aria-label="Panchayati Raj structure">
              <rect width="460" height="250" fill="#f8fafc" rx="8"/>
              
              <!-- Tier 1: Zilla Parishad (Top) -->
              <rect x="60" y="25" width="340" height="48" rx="6" fill="#0f766e"/>
              <text x="230" y="47" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12" font-weight="700" fill="#fff">Zilla Parishad (District Level)</text>
              <text x="230" y="62" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9" fill="#ccfbf1">Headed by Zilla Parishad Chairperson; MPs and MLAs are members</text>

              <!-- Arrow down -->
              <line x1="230" y1="73" x2="230" y2="92" stroke="#64748b" stroke-width="2"/>
              <polygon points="225,90 235,90 230,96" fill="#64748b"/>

              <!-- Tier 2: Panchayat Samiti (Block) -->
              <rect x="90" y="96" width="280" height="48" rx="6" fill="#0284c7"/>
              <text x="230" y="118" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12" font-weight="700" fill="#fff">Panchayat Samiti / Block / Mandal</text>
              <text x="230" y="133" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9" fill="#e0f2fe">Elected by all the Panchayat members in that block</text>

              <!-- Arrow down -->
              <line x1="230" y1="144" x2="230" y2="163" stroke="#64748b" stroke-width="2"/>
              <polygon points="225,161 235,161 230,167" fill="#64748b"/>

              <!-- Tier 3: Gram Panchayat (Village) -->
              <rect x="110" y="167" width="240" height="48" rx="6" fill="#10b981"/>
              <text x="230" y="189" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12" font-weight="700" fill="#fff">Gram Panchayat (Village Level)</text>
              <text x="230" y="204" text-anchor="middle" font-size="9" fill="#ecfdf5">Headed by Sarpanch; supervised by Gram Sabha</text>

              <text x="230" y="235" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10" fill="#64748b">Gram Sabha consists of all adult voters of the village; meets twice or thrice a year.</text>
            </svg>
          </div>
          <p class="visual-caption">Three-tier rural local self-government structure under the 73rd Constitutional Amendment.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Interactive 20-Question Quiz -->
    <section class="section">
      <h2>Interactive CBSE Board Exam Quiz (20 Questions)</h2>
      <p class="muted">Test your concepts on federal routes, legislative lists, language policies, and 1992 decentralisation with board exam standard MCQs.</p>

      <div class="quiz-container card" id="quiz-box">
        <div class="quiz-header-bar">
          <span class="quiz-counter" id="quiz-counter">Question 1 of 20</span>
          <span class="quiz-score" id="quiz-score">Score: 0 / 20</span>
        </div>

        <div class="quiz-question-body">
          <p class="quiz-q-text" id="quiz-question">Loading question...</p>
          <div class="quiz-options-list" id="quiz-options"></div>
          <div class="quiz-feedback" id="quiz-feedback"></div>
        </div>

        <div class="quiz-footer-actions">
          <button class="button secondary" id="quiz-prev-btn" disabled>← Previous</button>
          <button class="button" id="quiz-next-btn" disabled>Next Question →</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="quiz-completed-banner card" id="quiz-result-box" style="display:none; text-align:center; padding:2.5rem;">
        <h3>Quiz Completed! 🎉</h3>
        <p class="quiz-final-score" id="quiz-final-score" style="font-size:1.4rem; font-weight:700; color:var(--brand); margin:1rem 0;"></p>
        <p id="quiz-final-msg" class="muted"></p>
        <button class="button" id="quiz-restart-btn" style="margin-top:1rem;">Retake Quiz</button>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- CTA -->
    <section class="cta-banner">
      <h2>Need personalized guidance for Class 10 Social Science?</h2>
      <p>Book a free {provider.trial.duration_minutes}-minute trial session with {provider.tutor_name} (online or at-home) to master answer-writing frameworks and score 100% in CBSE Board Exams.</p>
      <a class="button" href={calBookingUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Book Free Trial</a>
    </section>
  </div>
</Layout>

<script is:inline>
(function() {
  const quizData = [
    {
      q: "Which of the following countries is an example of a 'Coming Together' federation?",
      options: ["India", "Spain", "USA", "Belgium"],
      answer: 2,
      explanation: "USA, Switzerland, and Australia are examples of 'Coming Together' federations where independent states voluntarily unite to form a bigger nation."
    },
    {
      q: "Which of the following countries is an example of a 'Holding Together' federation?",
      options: ["USA", "Australia", "India", "Switzerland"],
      answer: 2,
      explanation: "India, Spain, and Belgium are 'Holding Together' federations where a large sovereign country decides to divide power between the central government and states."
    },
    {
      q: "Which list of the Indian Constitution contains subjects like Defence, Foreign Affairs, and Banking?",
      options: ["State List", "Union List", "Concurrent List", "Residuary List"],
      answer: 1,
      explanation: "The Union List includes subjects of national importance like Defence, Foreign Affairs, Banking, Communications, and Currency."
    },
    {
      q: "On subjects included in the Concurrent List, who has the power to make laws?",
      options: ["Only the Union Government", "Only State Governments", "Both the Union and State Governments", "Local Panchayats"],
      answer: 2,
      explanation: "Both the Union and State governments can make laws on subjects in the Concurrent List. In case of a conflict between their laws, the Union law prevails."
    },
    {
      q: "Under which category do newly emerging subjects like 'Computer Software' and cyber laws fall?",
      options: ["Union List", "State List", "Concurrent List", "Residuary Subjects"],
      answer: 3,
      explanation: "Subjects that came up after the Constitution was made (like computer software and cyber laws) are considered Residuary Subjects and fall under the Union Government."
    },
    {
      q: "In case of a dispute regarding the division of legislative powers between Centre and States, who acts as the umpire?",
      options: ["The President", "The Prime Minister", "The High Courts and the Supreme Court", "The Parliament"],
      answer: 2,
      explanation: "The judiciary plays an essential role in overseeing the implementation of constitutional provisions. The High Courts and Supreme Court resolve disputes between levels of government."
    },
    {
      q: "What does the Indian Constitution say about a 'National Language'?",
      options: ["Hindi is declared as the National Language", "English is declared as the National Language", "Our Constitution did not give the status of national language to any one language", "Sanskrit is declared as the National Language"],
      answer: 2,
      explanation: "The Indian Constitution did not give the status of national language to any one language. Hindi was identified as the official language, but spoken by only about 40% of Indians."
    },
    {
      q: "Besides Hindi, how many other languages are recognized as 'Scheduled Languages' in the Eighth Schedule of the Indian Constitution?",
      options: ["18", "21", "22", "24"],
      answer: 1,
      explanation: "Besides Hindi, there are 21 other languages recognized as Scheduled Languages by the Constitution (making a total of 22 Scheduled Languages)."
    },
    {
      q: "The creation of Linguistic States in 1947 was the first and a major test for democratic politics in India. What was the outcome?",
      options: ["It led to the disintegration of India", "It actually made the country more united and administration easier", "It resulted in civil war between states", "It was revoked within 5 years"],
      answer: 1,
      explanation: "Experience has shown that the formation of linguistic states has actually made the country more united and administration much smoother."
    },
    {
      q: "In which year was a major constitutional amendment passed to deepen decentralisation to local government bodies?",
      options: ["1976", "1986", "1992", "2000"],
      answer: 2,
      explanation: "A major constitutional amendment was passed in 1992 (73rd and 74th Amendments) to make the third tier of democracy (Panchayati Raj and Municipalities) more powerful and effective."
    },
    {
      q: "What proportion of all positions in local government bodies is constitutionally reserved for women?",
      options: ["One-fourth (25%)", "One-third (33%)", "Half (50%)", "No reservation"],
      answer: 1,
      explanation: "At least one-third (33%) of all positions in rural and urban local government bodies are constitutionally reserved for women."
    },
    {
      q: "Which independent institution was created in each state to conduct panchayat and municipal elections?",
      options: ["Election Commission of India", "State Election Commission", "Planning Commission", "Finance Commission"],
      answer: 1,
      explanation: "An independent institution called the State Election Commission was created in each state to conduct regular panchayat and municipal elections."
    },
    {
      q: "Who is the political head of a Municipal Corporation in urban areas?",
      options: ["Municipal Commissioner", "Mayor", "Sarpanch", "District Magistrate (Collector)"],
      answer: 1,
      explanation: "In a Municipal Corporation, the elected political head is the Mayor (while the Municipal Commissioner is an appointed administrative officer)."
    },
    {
      q: "The Gram Panchayat works under the overall supervision of which body consisting of all adult voters of the village?",
      options: ["Zilla Parishad", "Gram Sabha", "Panchayat Samiti", "Block Development Officer"],
      answer: 1,
      explanation: "The Gram Panchayat works under the overall supervision of the Gram Sabha, which includes all adult voters of the village and must meet at least twice or thrice a year."
    },
    {
      q: "Which tier of government governs Union Territories like Chandigarh and Lakshadweep?",
      options: ["State Governments of neighboring states", "Central Government through Administrators/Lt. Governors", "Local Panchayats only", "Supreme Court directly"],
      answer: 1,
      explanation: "Union Territories are areas too small to become independent states but could not be merged with existing states. The Central Government has special powers in running these areas."
    },
    {
      q: "Which period saw the beginning of the era of Coalition Governments at the Centre in India?",
      options: ["1952", "1971", "1990", "2014"],
      answer: 2,
      explanation: "The period after 1990 saw the rise of regional political parties and the beginning of the era of coalition governments at the Centre, strengthening federal autonomy."
    },
    {
      q: "What is an essential feature of a Unitary System of government?",
      options: ["Power is divided between center and states", "Either there is only one level of government or sub-units are subordinate to the central government", "Judiciary has no power", "State governments can overrule national laws"],
      answer: 1,
      explanation: "Under the unitary system, either there is only one level of government or the sub-units are subordinate to the central government, which can pass orders to provincial bodies."
    },
    {
      q: "Which article of the Indian Constitution grants special provisions to states like Nagaland, Mizoram, and Assam due to their historical/indigenous circumstances?",
      options: ["Article 356", "Article 370", "Article 371", "Article 360"],
      answer: 2,
      explanation: "Article 371 of the Constitution grants special provisions to several states like Nagaland, Mizoram, Assam, and Arunachal Pradesh to protect indigenous cultures and customary laws."
    },
    {
      q: "How can fundamental provisions of the Indian Constitution regarding federal power sharing be amended?",
      options: ["By a simple majority in Lok Sabha", "By two-thirds majority in both Houses of Parliament AND ratification by legislatures of at least half of the total states", "By presidential ordinance", "By a decree of the Supreme Court"],
      answer: 1,
      explanation: "Any change to the constitutional division of powers requires a two-thirds majority in both Houses of Parliament and must be ratified by the legislatures of at least half of the total States."
    },
    {
      q: "Why can the real success of federalism in India be attributed to the nature of democratic politics?",
      options: ["Because of the strict constitutional text alone", "Because respect for diversity and desire for living together became shared ideals in our country", "Because single-party rule prevailed for 70 years", "Because of army enforcement"],
      answer: 1,
      explanation: "The real success of federalism in India is attributed to democratic politics, ensuring that mutual trust, respect for diversity, and desire for living together became shared national ideals."
    }
  ];

  let currentIdx = 0;
  let userAnswers = new Array(quizData.length).fill(null);

  const qText = document.getElementById("quiz-question");
  const optionsBox = document.getElementById("quiz-options");
  const feedbackBox = document.getElementById("quiz-feedback");
  const counterBox = document.getElementById("quiz-counter");
  const scoreBox = document.getElementById("quiz-score");
  const prevBtn = document.getElementById("quiz-prev-btn");
  const nextBtn = document.getElementById("quiz-next-btn");
  const quizBox = document.getElementById("quiz-box");
  const resultBox = document.getElementById("quiz-result-box");
  const finalScore = document.getElementById("quiz-final-score");
  const finalMsg = document.getElementById("quiz-final-msg");
  const restartBtn = document.getElementById("quiz-restart-btn");

  function renderQuestion(idx) {
    const item = quizData[idx];
    counterBox.textContent = `Question ${idx + 1} of ${quizData.length}`;
    qText.textContent = item.q;
    optionsBox.innerHTML = "";
    feedbackBox.textContent = "";
    feedbackBox.className = "quiz-feedback";

    const selected = userAnswers[idx];

    item.options.forEach((optText, oIdx) => {
      const btn = document.createElement("button");
      btn.className = "quiz-opt-btn";
      btn.textContent = `${String.fromCharCode(65 + oIdx)}. ${optText}`;

      if (selected !== null) {
        btn.disabled = true;
        if (oIdx === item.answer) {
          btn.classList.add("opt-correct");
        } else if (oIdx === selected) {
          btn.classList.add("opt-wrong");
        }
      } else {
        btn.onclick = () => selectOption(oIdx);
      }

      optionsBox.appendChild(btn);
    });

    if (selected !== null) {
      if (selected === item.answer) {
        feedbackBox.textContent = `✓ Correct! ${item.explanation}`;
        feedbackBox.classList.add("feedback-correct");
      } else {
        feedbackBox.textContent = `✗ Incorrect. Correct answer is option ${String.fromCharCode(65 + item.answer)}. ${item.explanation}`;
        feedbackBox.classList.add("feedback-wrong");
      }
    }

    prevBtn.disabled = idx === 0;
    nextBtn.disabled = selected === null;
    nextBtn.textContent = idx === quizData.length - 1 ? "Finish Quiz →" : "Next Question →";
    updateScoreDisplay();
  }

  function selectOption(oIdx) {
    userAnswers[currentIdx] = oIdx;
    renderQuestion(currentIdx);
  }

  function updateScoreDisplay() {
    let score = 0;
    userAnswers.forEach((ans, i) => {
      if (ans === quizData[i].answer) score++;
    });
    scoreBox.textContent = `Score: ${score} / ${quizData.length}`;
  }

  prevBtn.onclick = () => {
    if (currentIdx > 0) {
      currentIdx--;
      renderQuestion(currentIdx);
    }
  };

  nextBtn.onclick = () => {
    if (currentIdx < quizData.length - 1) {
      currentIdx++;
      renderQuestion(currentIdx);
    } else {
      finishQuiz();
    }
  };

  function finishQuiz() {
    quizBox.style.display = "none";
    resultBox.style.display = "block";
    let score = 0;
    userAnswers.forEach((ans, i) => {
      if (ans === quizData[i].answer) score++;
    });
    finalScore.textContent = `Your Final Score: ${score} / ${quizData.length} (${Math.round((score / quizData.length) * 100)}%)`;
    if (score >= 18) {
      finalMsg.textContent = "Outstanding mastery! You have exceptional clarity on Federalism, legislative distribution, and 1992 decentralisation.";
    } else if (score >= 14) {
      finalMsg.textContent = "Great job! Review coming together vs holding together federations and linguistic state history to score 100%.";
    } else {
      finalMsg.textContent = "Review the three lists and Panchayati Raj structure above, then re-attempt the quiz.";
    }
  }

  restartBtn.onclick = () => {
    userAnswers = new Array(quizData.length).fill(null);
    currentIdx = 0;
    resultBox.style.display = "none";
    quizBox.style.display = "block";
    renderQuestion(0);
  };

  renderQuestion(0);
})();
</script>

<style>
  .resource-header {
    padding-block: 1.5rem 2rem;
  }

  .clean-list {
    margin: 0.5rem 0 0;
    padding-left: 1.2rem;
    font-size: 0.92rem;
    color: var(--muted);
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  .clean-list li + li {
    margin-top: 0.35rem;
  }

  .formula {
    font-size: 1.15rem;
    font-weight: 800;
    color: var(--brand);
    margin: 0.4rem 0 0.6rem;
    font-family: inherit;
  }

  /* ── Visual Cards ── */
  .visual-card {
    padding: 0;
    overflow: hidden;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
  }

